        <p style="font-weight: 900; font-size: 20">Color (or colour in Commonwealth English; see spelling differences) is the visual perception based on the
        electromagnetic spectrum. Though color is not an inherent property of matter, color perception is related to an object's
        light absorption, reflection, emission spectra, and interference. For most humans, colors are perceived in the visible
        light spectrum with three types of cone cells (trichromacy). Other animals may have a different number of cone cell
        types or have eyes sensitive to different wavelengths, such as bees that can distinguish ultraviolet, and thus have a
        different color sensitivity range. Animal perception of color originates from different light wavelength or spectral
        sensitivity in cone cell types, which is then processed by the brain.</p>
